From 8bf930d41c6cf6d8dcce1b39fcaffebdd0f3f0a8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 21:20:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] MultiviewChoice: the record of a multiview profile declares
 the second view

A box 'mvcC' counted as the declaration of a second view only when a
subset sequence parameter set of a multiview profile was found among
the sets of its record. That takes the layout of those sets on trust,
and there is no file here to read it from: a file whose record is laid
out a little differently would have been taken for a plain H.264 file
and played in 2D, with nothing in the log.

The record names its profile in its second byte. One that names the
Multiview High or the Stereo High profile now declares a second view by
that alone; the sets are only looked at when it names another profile.
The box still has to lie within the data, behind the parameter sets of
the base view, and its record has to be of version 1: the four letters
alone still count for nothing.

Should such a file have no second view that the decoder gives, the
decoder side goes back to the base view by itself.

The host test has 230 checks now.
---
 xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/MultiviewChoice.h | 20 ++++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/MultiviewChoice.h b/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/MultiviewChoice.h
index fcde67c..622befc 100644
--- a/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/MultiviewChoice.h
+++ b/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/MultiviewChoice.h
@@ -78,16 +78,20 @@ inline uint64_t Be32(const uint8_t* data)
 /*!
  * \brief Whether a record of the form of the 'mvcC' box declares a second view
  *
- * It is laid out as the record of 'avcC': six bytes, a count of sequence parameter sets and
- * each of them behind its length. Those of a second view are subset sequence parameter sets
- * (NAL unit type 15) of the Multiview High or the Stereo High profile; one is enough. The count
- * has seven bits here, where 'avcC' has five below three bits that are set: a writer that kept
- * those three bits is understood too.
+ * It is laid out as the record of 'avcC': its version, the profile it is of and four bytes
+ * more, a count of sequence parameter sets and each of them behind its length. The record of a
+ * second view names the Multiview High or the Stereo High profile, and that is enough. One that
+ * names another profile still declares a second view when one of its sets is a subset sequence
+ * parameter set (NAL unit type 15) of one of those two profiles. The count has seven bits here,
+ * where 'avcC' has five below three bits that are set: a writer that kept those three bits is
+ * understood too.
  */
 inline bool DeclaresSecondView(const uint8_t* record, size_t size)
 {
   if (size < 7 || record[0] != 1)
     return false;
+  if (record[1] == PROFILE_MULTIVIEW_HIGH || record[1] == PROFILE_STEREO_HIGH)
+    return true;
 
   const unsigned int count = (record[5] & 0xe0) == 0xe0 ? record[5] & 0x1f : record[5] & 0x7f;
   bool found = false;
@@ -118,9 +122,9 @@ inline bool DeclaresSecondView(const uint8_t* record, size_t size)
  *
  * The record of the base view is walked to the end of its parameter sets, and the box is looked
  * for from there on (the record may go on with a few bytes that a writer is free to leave out,
- * so the place of the box is not fixed). A box counts when it lies within the data and holds a
- * subset sequence parameter set of a multiview profile: the four letters alone, which could
- * stand anywhere, do not.
+ * so the place of the box is not fixed). A box counts when it lies within the data and its
+ * record is one of a multiview profile, or holds a subset sequence parameter set of one: the
+ * four letters alone, which could stand anywhere, do not.
  */
 inline bool HasMvcConfiguration(const uint8_t* data, size_t size)
 {
